{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgfn13trrUec1WcH4f17J","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Permanganate can't be analysed with a d–d ligand-field fit, so I'm not giving Dq or B.\n\n- **Electron count:** Mn(VII) in MnO₄⁻ is d⁰. A Tanabe–Sugano analysis needs d² to d⁸ and a set of d–d transitions. A d⁰ ion has no d electrons, so it has no d–d bands and no ligand-field terms to fit.\n- **Origin of the bands:** The bands at 17 570, 31 560 and 38 320 cm⁻¹ are ligand-to-metal charge-transfer (LMCT) transitions. They go from O 2p-based orbitals (t₁, t₂) to the Mn 3d orbitals (e, t₂). The assignments are ¹A₁ → ¹T₂ (t₁→e), then t₂→e, then t₁→t₂.\n- **Intensity:** The molar absorptivities of about 1 500–3 000 M⁻¹ cm⁻¹ are far too high for d–d bands in a centrosymmetric complex. They are also high for the d–d bands of a tetrahedral complex, which are typically about 100 M⁻¹ cm⁻¹. This is consistent with charge transfer.\n- **Parameters:** The tetrahedral splitting Δt is not defined by these LMCT energies. They mix ligand and metal orbital energies. B and C are Racah parameters for d–d electron repulsion, and a d⁰ ion has none. Any values I gave would be arbitrary.\n\nThe ligand-field model cannot reliably determine Dq or B here.\n\n{\"Dq\": null, \"B\": null, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": true, \"reason\": \"MnO4- is d0 tetrahedral and its bands are LMCT, not d-d, so Dq and B cannot be determined from a ligand-field fit.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":253,"cache_read_input_tokens":7806,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":253,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4575b68d-337b-4688-a26b-c041f2b418